It seemed to check a lot of the right boxes:
* Weather-sealed enclosure for harsh environments.
* Durable casing that can take some knocking around.
* Enough internal space to fit node hardware without having a ton of excess volume.
* A solar setup that didn’t depend on a perfectly aimed panel.

That last one mattered more than I expected with my previous solar tree builds. Awkward panel mounts could throw off the balance of the node when hanging, and panels facing the wrong direction or twisting in the wind could give you subpar charging performance.

The clear case and triangular solar panel arrangement mean it can collect light from multiple sides. Your placement becomes a lot more forgiving once you have it deployed.



## Features:
    • Highly robust & weather-sealed.
    • Effective tri-panel solar setup that can produce in low light.
    • Comes with 18650 battery already installed. (quality may vary)
    • Depending on variant, JST 2-pin connectors on battery and panel. 
    • Drop tested onto grass from over 50 feet, twice, with no damage to the case or internals.
    • Multiple options for mounting using side anchor points.



## Cost: 
At the time of writing, these run $8 to $12 on average depending on vendor (you can frequently can use new-buyer code on AliExpress to get the first one for $8 or less)

Tools and Materials Needed:
    • Large wrench or other similar tool. (for cap removal)
    • Heat gun or hair dryer.
    • Small Phillips screwdriver.
    • Soldering iron and wire cutters. (if device has soldered solar connection)
    • Drill and drill bit sized to the antenna connector type you are installing. (if using an external antenna)
    • Electrical tape. (optional)
    • PTFE thread tape. (optional)
    • Li-Protect board (optional, but recommended to avoid brownouts etc)
      
## Node Selection:
Note: The best devices are the smaller nRF-based nodes.

XIAO nRF52840 & Wio SX1262 Kit:
This will fit inside with ample room. Requires soldering a JST pigtail or wires directly.

Heltec T096: Great fit if you can snugly tuck it down between the panels, low power consumption, solar support, higher power RF output than some other choices.

Philly Mesh Micro Node (PMMN):
Fits extremely well inside the “triangle” of the solar panels. Out of the box, the orientation of the +/- polarity on the JST 2.0P connectors lined up perfectly with the board’s markings.

RAK19003+4631:
Will work, but will require careful placement of the components to avoid damaging them when screwing the cap back on. The stock JST 2.0P cable for the solar connection is smaller than what the buoy has (if yours came with a JST cable for solar). It will need to be modified, or a new lead soldered to the panels.

Note: during testing a full-sized RAK19007 will not fit inside an unmodified casing.

Warning:
There are a couple of variants out there. The biggest variant to watch for uses a press-fit cap that does not unscrew. Avoid this one. Examine the vendor pictures so you know which version you are receiving.

There are also versions with a loop at the center of one end and versions without (see photo). I’ve noticed the ones without the loop also have the solar panel connector soldered to the LED board. This may add soldering or splicing work later, but depending on what LoRa board you go with that may have already been in your future.



## Disassembly:
The device comes with a magnet that is used to activate the LED system. This is not needed and can be discarded / stuck to your fridge.

All of the buoys I have purchased have come with the caps heavily sealed with silicone sealant/adhesive. The best method I have found is to carefully heat the threaded portion of the cap with low to medium heat to soften the adhesive. Use caution here, as you do not want to use a high enough heat level to melt or deform the outer case plastic.

Take caution and slowly rotate the buoy in front of the heating device, this could take a few minutes. Afterward, gently but firmly attempt to twist off the cap with a wrench. You may need to put it back under heat if it doesn’t break loose. It will require some decent force to get it started.

Afterward, I like to clean out the threads to make it easier to take the cap on and off. With the rubber O-ring provided on the cap, I have had no issues with water intrusion through multiple storms. For extra security, you could apply PTFE thread tape.

Once the cap is removed, you should have a clear view of the LED system. You can see if it has two JST two-pin connectors or the single connector. The LED board should have two to three small Phillips screws holding it down. Carefully remove them and disconnect the board.

The LED board itself will not be used and can be discarded or used for salvaged components for another project.

At this point, I like to carefully remove the triangle-shaped solar panel and battery assembly and unfold it so it lies flat.

I like to adjust any foam spacers on the bottom, moving it down so it is flush with the bottom of the panels and case. You can then shift the 18650 battery so it is resting on the foam. This opens up some spare room for additional wiring.

For good measure I like to apply some electrical tape to any exposed contacts on the panels or battery. This is to prevent any shorts or other issues once deployed. I find it provides a small measure of peace of mind.



## Antenna:
If you are installing an external antenna, you’ll need to drill the casing. I’ve found the most flexible location is the removable cap. This allows you to change out the cap for a different mount type later (upgrading from SMA to N-type, etc.)
If you choose another location, like the other end keep in mind you will need a longer U.FL pigtail. 

## Assembly:
You are now ready to lay out your components for installation. Connect up the antenna lead first (and make sure you have an antenna on it!) Never power your nodes without an antenna attached kids!

Carefully connect up your Li-Protect board (also highly recommended), 18650 battery, and then solar connection. 

If all went according to plan your node should be powered up and ready to go. Now would be a good time to (carefully!) move the casing out to a sunny spot (or under a bright lamp) to ensure it looks like the panels are charging.

With the node safely  nestled down in between the panels, slowly and carefully align the cap and twist it on. Your U.fl pigtail cable will corkscrew a bit as you twist the lid on but if you chose the appropriate length and your node doesn’t stick to far into the cap it should not be an issue. Go slow, don’t force anything. If the cap binds up on the board (this can happen with the RAK19003) back it out and reseat everything, you don’t want to bend or break the node.

## Mounting:
Nearly there! If everything went well up to this point, you should be the owner of a brand-new solar node that looks something like this.

-[picture]-

Let’s start with deploying in a tree or hanging from a line. We will assume you have your line deployed in your preferred fashion. With your antenna pointing down, affix your line to the uppermost of the side mounts. If your buoy casing also has a loop on the top, I like to run the line through that. The top loop is thinner material, so I don’t use that as the main anchor point, but only to help center the node on the line and for strain relief.
The line then goes up over the tree, back down to the ground and attaches to the bottom side attachment, closest to the cap.

Think of it as a continuous line with both ends terminating at opposite ends of the buoy. This ensures, like a flag, you can raise and lower it at will as needed. I’ve seen several tree-based solar node builds get stuck in trees when bark, sap, or debris snags a line and gravity doesn’t want to help bring it down. You will want it to come back down as needed. A well-placed cleat at the bottom of the tree or nearby fence provides an anchor point for tying it off..

The side mounting tabs also have a slight curve to them, which makes it very easy to zip-tie or strap the node to a PVC pipe, EMT tubing, etc.




